---
/**
 * faith.astro — /faith
 *
 * The statements of faith, under a cross. Reached from the cross on the
 * swell in the Caprock footer and the small cross in its basement line.
 *
 * THE PAGE, from the top:
 *
 *   the cross      a jewelled cross (crux gemmata) in a starry heaven,
 *                  after the apse of Sant'Apollinare in Classe at Ravenna:
 *                  the page's Latin cross in gold, set with rubies,
 *                  sapphires and emeralds and edged with pearls, on a
 *                  mosaic disc of blue sky and gold stars ringed in gold
 *                  and red. It is lit by the real sun: the header's sun,
 *                  read off it as the footer reads it. A glint crosses the
 *                  gold through the day, from the right in the morning to
 *                  the left at evening (the site looks north, as the
 *                  footer does), warming as the sun gets low; after dark
 *                  the gold and the sky dim and a candle's light rises
 *                  from the foot. Without the header it rests at
 *                  mid-morning.
 *   the creeds     the Apostles' and the Nicene, set as a prayer book sets
 *                  them: rubrics (the numeral, the Latin name, the closing
 *                  Amen) in red, an illuminated initial, and the rest of
 *                  the first line in small capitals. Side by side on a
 *                  wide screen, one after the other on a narrower one.
 *                  Each line keeps its line break and indent as written.
 *   the margin     beside the lines, the passages of Scripture they rest
 *                  on. Each reference opens the verse beneath the line, in
 *                  the King James Version (whose English the Apostles'
 *                  Creed here shares: "sitteth", "the quick and the
 *                  dead"), with a link to read it in context.
 *   my faith       a register: dated entries, one row each, in the order
 *                  they happened. Add an entry to REGISTER for each.
 *   the ribbon     a silk marker hanging from the top of the page, in the
 *                  colour of the church year's season today: violet,
 *                  white, red or green. The season is reckoned in
 *                  src/data/church-year.ts, when the page is built and
 *                  again in the browser, so it changes on its own; the
 *                  header's ?debugTime= override moves it too. Its name
 *                  shows on hover, or on a tap.
 *   the note       on the texts, at the foot: where each creed comes from,
 *                  and the Bible the margin quotes. Edit it in TEXT_NOTE.
 *   in print       the creeds alone in black and red on white, the
 *                  references kept as plain text, each creed on its page.
 *   the leaf       the page is a leaf of vellum hanging from the foot of
 *                  the header: a warm skin (a baked tile, public/images/
 *                  faith/vellum.webp), stains and browned edges, and the
 *                  scribe's double margin rules in red
 *                  down both sides. (No ruled lines across: lines that
 *                  did not fall exactly under the text would look wrong,
 *                  and the creeds' lines are of several sizes.)
 *   the window     sunlight through a Venetian window falls across the top
 *                  of the leaf: a bifora, two round-arched lights side by
 *                  side under one arch, with a quatrefoil in the
 *                  tympanum. Each light is jewel glass in the season's
 *                  colour with a gold cross, Alpha in one and Omega in
 *                  the other ("I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the
 *                  ending", Rev. 1:8). Its arches keep its light from
 *                  echoing the round heaven of the cross. It is
 *                  drawn once (WINDOW, below) and coloured by the
 *                  stylesheet, so the season recolours it; the sun moves
 *                  it, long from the right in the morning, short at noon,
 *                  long to the left at evening, and it fades at dusk.
 *   the mosaic     after St Mark's in Venice, the leaf stands in gold
 *                  mosaic: a gold lintel over it and, on a wide screen, a
 *                  gold pillar either side, each with a vine rising from
 *                  a chalice past three early Christian panels (the dove
 *                  with an olive branch, after the Domitilla catacomb; the
 *                  Good Shepherd; the fish bearing the basket of loaves,
 *                  after San Callisto). One border frames all of it, a
 *                  white rule, a red band set with gold diamonds and a
 *                  blue rule, with a red block at every corner. The
 *                  mosaic frames the leaf and stays out of it: between the
 *                  head of the page and the creeds there is only a scribe's
 *                  rubric, Credo, between two rules. All of it is baked to
 *                  small images in public/images/faith/, so it costs
 *                  nothing to draw; on a phone the pillars give way to the
 *                  leaf.
 *   the gilding    each creed's initial is gilded the first time it
 *                  scrolls into view: the gold is laid across red bole,
 *                  the clay gilders lay under leaf, and catches the light
 *                  once. With no script, or reduced motion, it is simply
 *                  gold. The initials stand raised, like leaf on the skin.
 *
 * TO EDIT A CREED, edit its text below. A blank line starts a new stanza;
 * leading spaces indent a line. "Amen." is set as a rubric wherever it
 * appears. A margin note names a line by its exact words (without the
 * indent); if a line is edited so that a note no longer finds it, or
 * finds it twice, the build stops and says which.
 */
import RootLayout from '../layouts/RootLayout.astro';
import { seasonOn } from '../data/church-year';

